      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s name comes from the Horizon Corporation, a real‑estate developer that began buying land in the 1960s and marketed lots across the southwestern United States.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizon_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Only the area around Horizon Boulevard and Kenazo Street succeeded, becoming the core of Horizon City while surrounding subdivisions remain largely undeveloped.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizon_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Because of the 1994 Texas Colonia Act, many eastern lots cannot be legally sold or developed without guaranteed water, sewer, and utility access.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizon_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A local homeowners’ association, the Horizon Communities Improvement Association, is working to consolidate undeveloped lots into tracts that can meet state development requirements.",
